---
/* One pilot, in one of four SHAPES.

   Round 4 ranked the four cards by height and gave 03 a different anatomy,
   and the critic named what was still wrong: under every band sat the same
   209px PROBLEM / APPROACH / GOAL slab, four times, at the same measure. The
   moment each pilot should have become specific was the moment they all
   became one object.

   So the argument itself is now composed per pilot. The GRAMMAR is fixed —
   index · partner · sector, a serif name, one line of tagline, and the three
   fields at one line each — but the SHAPE that grammar is set in is not:

     01  ANCHOR   a near-screen band, held at the lightest grade on the page
                  so the photograph is actually SEEN, then the argument
                  DESCENDS: the setup small and left, a held pause bounded by
                  its rule and by the brass label under it, and the outcome
                  alone under it. This pilot is the thesis.
     02  ASIDE    no band at all. The photograph bleeds off the left edge at
                  five-quarters and the whole argument stands beside it in a
                  ruled column — the compressed one, and the one that breaks
                  the run of full-bleed frames.
     03  TURN     the name climbs to the top of the frame and the three
                  fields are set ON the photograph along its foot. No ground
                  beneath it at all: the fastest block on the page.
     04  CLOSE    the series closes reversed — outcome first, the setup that
                  produced it after it as a small dim note. The reversal is
                  carried by WEIGHT, not by right-alignment, so it reads as
                  deliberate rather than scrambled.

   Held constant so the four still read as one commission: the palette, the
   nameplate grammar, the three micro registers, and one line per field. */
import Figure from './Figure.astro';
import Scale from './pilots-scale.astro';

const {
  pilot: p,
  shape = 'close',
  grade = 0.6,
  focal = '50% 50%',
  parallax = 5,
  loading = 'lazy',
} = Astro.props;

const hid = `${p.id}-h`;
const isAside = shape === 'aside';

/* The ranking is DESCENDED, not declared once. Four shapes, four band
   heights and now four rungs of the ladder, one step apart the whole way
   down. Round 6 moved the anchor's own NAME down to --step-head and gave
   --step-poster to its GOAL instead: the loudest mark in card 01 is now the
   outcome, not the label on the pilot that produced it. Loudest-per-card
   descends 86 → 67 → 53 → 34 at 1440. Card 02 carries --step-feature, the
   page's one series-breaker — it is also the card that breaks the run of
   full-bleed frames and the one plate held at full exposure. Card 04 closes
   at --step-sub, the small serif display rung: a coda, set quietly. */
/* The descent above is the intent; this map has to actually produce it.
   `turn` was on --step-poster, so card 03's name came in a rung ABOVE card
   02's — which is the card nominated as the page's one series-breaker. The
   run measured 86 → 67 → 86 → 34: the breaker was not the loudest thing in
   the series it was breaking, and /pilots/ carried three --step-poster lines
   (masthead, card 01's goal, card 03's name) instead of the two it means to.
   --step-head restores the ladder the comment describes. */
const rungs = {
  anchor: 'display--head',
  aside:  'display--feature',
  turn:   'display--head',
  close:  'display--sub',
};
const rung = rungs[shape] ?? 'display--head';

/* ── Where the ink goes ───────────────────────────────────────────────
   Until now this card painted its own top and bottom ramps (.band::before /
   ::after) AND took Figure's default scrim, and the strip painted a third.
   Two ink systems stacked on one photograph is what flattened the foot of
   pilot-maternal to L* 3.5 and drew a hard grey seam straight across a
   mother's face. There is now exactly ONE: Figure's, placed per card, and
   named here because placement is a composition decision.

     01 anchor  type along the whole bottom edge → a low full-width ramp
     02 aside   nothing written on it but the corner credit → a corner
     03 turn    name at the TOP → ink at the top, none at the bottom, so the
                photograph's lower two thirds are simply seen
     04 close   nothing written on it at all → no ink but the bar strip.

   Which is also the page's other order: 01 sets its type INTO the picture,
   02 beside it, 03 at the top of it, 04 underneath it. Four relationships
   between a sentence and a photograph, in the order the argument runs.  */
const SCRIM = {
  anchor: { scrim: 'bottom', reach: 1.00, rise: 0.42, ink: 0.94, vig: 0.55 },
  aside:  { scrim: 'bottom-left', reach: 0.42, rise: 0.30, ink: 0.9, vig: 0.35 },
  turn:   { scrim: 'bottom-left', reach: 0.46, rise: 0.30, ink: 0.92, vig: 0.30 },
  close:  { scrim: 'none', vig: 0.35 },
};
const sk = SCRIM[shape] ?? SCRIM.close;
---
<article class:list={['pcard', `pcard--${shape}`]} id={p.id} aria-labelledby={hid}>
  {isAside ? (
    <div class="aside">
      <!-- TERRITORY, then WEIGHT: the plate is uncovered rather than faded,
           and then it keeps responding — the one photograph on this page held
           at full exposure is also the one that answers the reader's scroll. -->
      <div class="aside__fig" data-wipe="left">
        <div class="aside__plate" data-settle="0.5">
        <Figure
          src={p.image} alt="" ratio="auto" fill={true}
          grade={grade} focal={focal} parallax={parallax} loading={loading}
          credit={p.credit} creditPos="bottom-left"
          scrim={sk.scrim} reach={sk.reach} rise={sk.rise} ink={sk.ink} vig={sk.vig}
        />
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="aside__side" data-seq="120" data-seq-from="180">
        <p class="rail" data-reveal>
          <span class="index">{p.index}</span>
          <span class="eyebrow rail__partner">{p.partner}</span>
          <span class="label rail__field">{p.field}</span>
        </p>

        <div class="say" data-reveal>
          <h2 class:list={['display', rung, 'say__t', 'lines']} id={hid}>{p.title}</h2>
          <p class="say__tag">{p.tagline}</p>
        </div>

        <dl class="arg arg--side" data-reveal>
          <div><dt class="label">Problem</dt><dd>{p.problem}</dd></div>
          <div><dt class="label">Approach</dt><dd>{p.approach}</dd></div>
          <div class="arg__out">
            <dt class="label label--brass">Goal</dt>
            <dd class="bridge bridge--fg">{p.goal}</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>
  ) : (
    <div class="band">
      <!-- WHAT THE PHOTOGRAPH IS. A pilot's name set across a full-bleed frame
           reads as that frame's caption whether or not it was meant as one, and
           three of these four photographs are of somewhere and something other
           than the pilot they illustrate — most sharply card 01, where a
           Unified track event carries the words "Healthy Athletes" and "a free
           screening". refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md names that exact misreading as the
           one this site must not make. The credit is the house remedy and it
           already runs on the two other full-bleed scenes. -->
      <Figure
        src={p.image} alt="" ratio="auto"
        grade={grade} focal={focal} parallax={parallax} loading={loading}
        credit={p.credit} creditPos={shape === 'turn' ? 'bottom-right' : 'bottom-right'}
        scrim={sk.scrim} reach={sk.reach} rise={sk.rise} ink={sk.ink} vig={sk.vig}
      />

      <p class="shell rail" data-reveal>
        <span class="index">{p.index}</span>
        <span class="eyebrow rail__partner">{p.partner}</span>
        <span class="label rail__field">{p.field}</span>
      </p>

      <!-- a TRIGGER, not a fade: neutralised below, so the masked name lands
           on the earlier threshold and the tagline is delayed off that beat -->
      {shape !== 'close' && (
        <div class="shell say" data-reveal>
          <h2 class:list={['display', 'say__t', 'lines', rung]} id={hid}>{p.title}</h2>
          {shape === 'turn' && <span class="turn__lead" aria-hidden="true" data-wipe="left" data-delay="520"></span>}
          {shape !== 'turn' && <p class="say__tag">{p.tagline}</p>}
        </div>
      )}

      <!-- 03 only. The name is set at poster size ACROSS the top of the frame
           and the one supporting line is set at its FOOT, in the corner the
           ramp protects. Between them is nothing but the photograph, at
           near-screen height: type at both ends, picture in the middle. -->
      {shape === 'turn' && (
        <p class="shell turn__foot bridge" data-reveal="240">{p.tagline}</p>
      )}

    </div>
  )}

  {/* 03's argument used to sit ON the frame, mid-picture, over a lit wall at
      L* 63, with its own ramp cutting a grey seam across the photograph. It
      comes off. What is left on the picture is the bar rail and the name,
      inside the top band the system guarantees — and the lower two thirds of
      the frame, which is the whole photograph, is handed back. */}
  {shape === 'turn' && (
    <dl class="shell arg arg--row" data-wipe="left" data-delay="200">
      <div><dt class="label">Problem</dt><dd>{p.problem}</dd></div>
      <div><dt class="label">Approach</dt><dd>{p.approach}</dd></div>
      <div class="arg__out"><dt class="label label--brass">Goal</dt><dd class="bridge bridge--fg">{p.goal}</dd></div>
    </dl>
  )}

  <!-- ── THE PAGE TURNED OVER ──────────────────────────────────────────
       This page's one exception, spent once, on the one sentence it exists
       to make. Everything on this site is cream on navy; the institute
       talking. Pilot 01's outcome is not the institute talking — it is the
       thing that will be true or not true in ninety days, and the thing the
       page promises to publish either way. So it is PRINTED: full bleed,
       navy on cream, edge to edge, immediately after the largest photograph
       on the page. One tonal event, at the top of the argument, and then the
       page closes back to ground and stays there for pilots 02, 03 and 04.
       Ground budget (tokens.css): photography + this. Nothing else. -->
  {shape === 'anchor' && (
    <div class="print on-cream">
    <div class="shell arg arg--descent">
      <span class="arg__rule" aria-hidden="true" data-wipe="center"></span>

      <!-- PROBLEM and APPROACH are two halves of one sentence, so they are
           set as two adjacent columns under one hairline with a rule between
           them, not thrown to opposite edges of the page. -->
      <!-- SETUP AND INSTRUMENT, one block. The two clauses and the axis that
           measures them used to be three polite bands with 119px of dead
           field between the first two and the third stranded under the
           outcome. They are one object now: what the problem is, what we
           will do, and the window we are accountable inside — read together,
           in the top half of the sheet. -->
      <div class="arg__top">
        <dl class="arg__setup" data-seq="150" data-seq-from="80">
          <div data-reveal><dt class="label">Problem</dt><dd>{p.problem}</dd></div>
          <div data-reveal><dt class="label">Approach</dt><dd>{p.approach}</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- THE SENTENCE, SET THROUGH THE AXIS.
         The outcome is a claim about a quantity, so the page stops stating
         the quantity in words and draws it: a twelve-month axis at the full
         width of the viewport, and the sentence set across it. The head runs
         from the page's own left margin; the tail begins at 25% — the closing
         edge of the ninety-day span and the x of the numeral 3. You do not
         read that ninety days is a quarter of the window. You see it. -->
    <div class="measure">
      <p class="label label--brass measure__gl" data-reveal>Goal</p>
      <p class="display display--poster measure__g" data-reveal="90">{p.goalHead ?? p.goal}</p>
      <Scale />
      {p.goalTail && (
        <p class="display display--poster measure__g measure__g--hang" data-settle="0.62">{p.goalTail}</p>
      )}
    </div>
    </div>
  )}

  {shape === 'close' && (
    <div class="shell say say--ground" data-reveal>
      <h2 class:list={['display', 'say__t', 'lines', rung]} id={hid}>{p.title}</h2>
      <p class="say__tag">{p.tagline}</p>
    </div>
  )}

  <!-- ── THE SECOND GROUND EVENT ───────────────────────────────────────
       The page ran dark / cream / dark and then held one navy value for the
       whole back half — 52% of the scroll in a single tone with four photo
       bands cut into it. The coda now rests on a PLATE (--ink-700, L* 15):
       an object set down on the page, with its own edges, carrying the last
       outcome. tokens.css allows three ground changes and one cream; this is
       the third, and it is the one the page closes on. -->
  {shape === 'close' && (
    <div class="shell coda">
    <div class="arg arg--coda on-plate">
      <span class="arg__rule" aria-hidden="true" data-wipe="center"></span>
      <!-- The close reverses the series — outcome first, setup after — but it
           still reads left to right and top to bottom. The reversal is carried
           by weight: the goal at full cream on the bridge rung, the setup that
           produced it set small and dim beside it, as a note. -->
      <div class="arg__goal">
        <p class="label label--brass arg__gl" data-reveal>Goal</p>
        <p class="bridge bridge--fg arg__g" data-reveal="110">{p.goal}</p>
      </div>
      <dl class="arg__setup" data-seq="120" data-seq-from="200">
        <div data-reveal><dt class="label">Problem</dt><dd>{p.problem}</dd></div>
        <div data-reveal><dt class="label">Approach</dt><dd>{p.approach}</dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
    </div>
  )}
</article>

<style>
  /* overflow-x: clip, not hidden — it clips the strip's full-bleed ink ramp
     and the aside's left bleed without making the card a scroll container. */
  .pcard { scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--nav-h) + 0.5rem); position: relative; overflow-x: clip; }

  /* ── The band ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Four rows so the nameplate can sit at either end of the frame. */
  .band {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    /* The foot of the band belongs to the photograph's credit. The credit is
       painted by the absolutely-positioned figure, so this padding moves the
       say block and the strip UP off it rather than moving both together —
       without it the credit reads as a third line of the tagline, which is
       the caption misreading refs/PHOTO-FACTS.md exists to prevent. */
    padding-bottom: clamp(1.7rem, 3.4vw, 3rem);
    min-height: var(--band, clamp(19rem, 48vh, 28rem));
    display: grid;
    grid-template-rows: auto auto 1fr auto;
  }
  .pcard--anchor { --band: clamp(31rem, 86vh, 48rem); }
  .pcard--turn   { --band: clamp(28rem, 82vh, 44rem); }
  .pcard--close  { --band: clamp(17rem, 42vh, 25rem); }

  /* The band paints NO ink of its own. Figure carries the whole treatment
     now, placed per card by the SCRIM table above — one system, so nothing
     doubles and no ramp lands anywhere the type is not. */

  /* padding lives on the children: the fill Figure is positioned against the
     band's padding box, so padding here would inset the photograph. */
  .rail, .say { position: relative; z-index: 1; }

  /* ── Nameplate ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Three registers and nothing else: brass numeral, the partner as this
     card's one eyebrow, the sector as a dim label. */
  .rail {
    margin: 0;
    padding-top: clamp(1.35rem, 2.6vw, 2.25rem);
    display: flex;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 1.15ch;
  }
  .rail__field { margin-left: auto; text-align: right; white-space: nowrap; }

  .band > .rail { grid-row: 1; }
  .band > .say { grid-row: 4; }

  /* The tagline used to sit in a second column at the right of the frame —
     86% across, which on a directional ramp is exactly where the system
     hands the picture back, and on the track photograph is lit asphalt at
     L* 55. It comes down under the name, into the ink, and the right half of
     every frame stays a photograph. */
  .say {
    padding-bottom: clamp(1.75rem, 3.4vw, 3rem);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    row-gap: 0.75rem;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .say__t { line-height: 0.94; letter-spacing: -0.026em; text-wrap: initial; }
  /* the small serif display rung wants less negative tracking and more leading
     than the poster rung does */
  .pcard--close .say__t { line-height: 1.04; letter-spacing: -0.012em; }
  /* the coda's name is the quietest on the page; the note beside it has to be
     quieter still, or the ranking inverts inside the card */
  /* 04's tagline used to sit in the right column, which on this photograph
     is the welding flash — L* 55 under a 70% register. It comes down under
     the name instead, into the ramp, and the light stays in the picture. */
  .pcard--close .say__tag { max-width: 30ch; padding-bottom: 0; }
  .pcard--turn .say__t { line-height: 0.98; letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  .say__tag {
    margin: 0;
    padding-bottom: 0;
    font-size: var(--step-lede);
    line-height: 1.45;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* ── THE PRINT: the page turned over ──────────────────────────────
     Full bleed, navy on cream, once on this page. It lands directly off the
     foot of the largest photograph on the site, so the change of value is
     also a change of medium: a photograph, then a printed page. */
  .print {
    /* the deep top rule is the seam between the picture and the page */
    border-top: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--ink-900) 22%, transparent);
    padding-block: clamp(1.6rem, 3vw, 2.6rem) clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4.25rem);
  }
  /* the block's own top padding is the print band's now */
  .print .arg--descent { padding-top: 0; padding-bottom: 0; }
  .print { padding-bottom: clamp(2.6rem, 5vw, 4.5rem); }

  /* ── 01 ANCHOR: the argument descends ─────────────────────────────
     Setup small and left under a hairline; then a held pause, bounded above
     by that rule and below by the brass label; then the outcome alone at the
     page's one --step-feature. */
  .arg--descent {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    padding-top: clamp(1.4rem, 2.6vw, 2.1rem);
    padding-bottom: 0;
  }
  .arg__rule {
    grid-column: 1 / -1;
    height: 1px; background: var(--rule);
    margin-bottom: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.6rem);
  }
  /* two halves of one argument: adjacent, sharing a gutter rule, held to the
     left two thirds so the pause below them is a held rest and not a margin */
  .arg--descent .arg__setup {
    margin: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 24rem) minmax(0, 24rem);
    column-gap: clamp(1.75rem, 3.4vw, 3.25rem);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .arg--descent .arg__setup > div + div {
    border-left: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
    padding-left: clamp(1.75rem, 3.4vw, 3.25rem);
    margin-left: calc(clamp(1.75rem, 3.4vw, 3.25rem) * -1);
  }

  /* the setup and its instrument, ruled together: two clauses across the top,
     the twelve-month axis under them at the same measure. One block. */
  .arg--descent .arg__top {
    display: grid;
    row-gap: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.4rem);
  }


  /* ── THE MEASURE: the axis, and the sentence set through it ────────
     Everything here is composed against the VIEWPORT, not against the text
     column — that is the exception, and it is spent once, on the one object
     this site has invented. --shell-x is the page's own left margin, so the
     head of the sentence still starts where every other line on the page
     starts; only the axis and the hanging tail are viewport-measured. */
  .print { --shell-x: max(var(--gutter), calc((100vw - var(--max)) / 2 + var(--gutter))); }
  .measure {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    margin-top: clamp(1.9rem, 4vw, 3.4rem);
  }
  .measure__gl { margin: 0 0 clamp(0.5rem, 1vw, 0.8rem); padding-left: var(--shell-x); }
  .measure__g {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: none;
    color: var(--fg);
    line-height: 0.98;
    letter-spacing: -0.024em;
    text-wrap: initial;
    padding-left: var(--shell-x);
    --settle-origin: 0% 100%;
    --settle-shift: 2.4vh;
  }
  /* the axis is the space between the two halves of the sentence */
  .measure :global(.mscale) { margin-block: clamp(0.7rem, 1.6vw, 1.25rem) 0; }
  /* …and the tail hangs off the closing edge of the ninety-day span */
  .measure__g--hang { padding-left: 25%; margin-top: clamp(0.15rem, 0.5vw, 0.4rem); }

  /* 04's nameplate stands on ground, under an open photograph. */
  .say--ground {
    padding-top: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.4rem);
    padding-bottom: clamp(0.9rem, 1.8vw, 1.4rem);
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 22rem);
    column-gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 5rem);
    align-items: end;
  }
  .say--ground .say__tag { padding-bottom: 0.35em; }

  /* ── 04 CLOSE: the coda — the argument read backwards ─────────────
     Outcome first at full cream, the setup after it as one small dim note.
     Everything still reads left-to-right; the reversal is in the weight. */
  .coda { padding-top: clamp(1rem, 2.2vw, 1.9rem); padding-bottom: clamp(1rem, 2.4vw, 2rem); }
  .arg--coda {
    display: grid;
    /* the outcome used to be capped at 22ch inside a 1.35fr track, which
       left 660px of empty plate between it and the note at the right edge —
       an unfilled table, not a pause. The claim now runs its own track. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(1.25rem, 0.1fr) minmax(0, 30rem);
    align-items: start;
    /* a plate has edges. It is set down on the page, not bled off it. */
    background: var(--bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
    padding: clamp(1.5rem, 3vw, 2.5rem) clamp(1.5rem, 3.4vw, 3rem) clamp(1.75rem, 3.4vw, 2.75rem);
  }
  .arg--coda .arg__rule { margin-bottom: clamp(1.1rem, 2.2vw, 1.8rem); }
  .arg--coda .arg__goal {
    grid-column: 1; display: grid; gap: clamp(0.7rem, 1.4vw, 1.05rem);
  }
  .arg--coda .arg__g { max-width: 34ch; }
  .arg--coda .arg__setup {
    grid-column: 3; margin: 0;
    display: grid; gap: clamp(0.65rem, 1.3vw, 0.95rem);
    align-content: start;
    /* the note is set against the goal, and the hairline says so */
    border-left: 1px solid var(--rule-soft);
    padding-left: clamp(1rem, 2vw, 1.6rem);
  }
  .arg--coda .arg__setup dd { max-width: 30ch; }

  /* ── 03 TURN: the page's one piece of type set AT the picture ───────
     Every other name on this page is a caption in a corner — 53px of ink in
     a 750,000px frame. This one is the exception the page is allowed. The
     name runs at --step-poster, the same rung as the printed GOAL, straight
     across the top of a near-screen frame and over the dark left of the
     photograph rather than politely beside it; the one supporting line is
     set at the frame's FOOT, in the corner the ramp protects. Between them
     is the photograph, whole, for two thirds of the height of the screen.
     Type at both ends, picture in the middle — and nothing else on the page
     is composed this way. */
  .pcard--turn .band > .say {
    grid-row: 2;
    align-self: start;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    padding-top: clamp(1.4rem, 3vw, 2.6rem);
    padding-bottom: 0;
    row-gap: 0;
    align-items: start;
  }
  /* the measure is the frame, not a column: the name is allowed to run into
     the picture, and where the inked letters stop a hairline carries the line
     the rest of the way to the edge — the same leader the masthead index
     uses, so the top of this frame is one mark 1337px wide rather than a
     577px caption with void beside it. */
  .pcard--turn .band > .say {
    grid-template-columns: auto minmax(1.5rem, 1fr);
    align-items: baseline;
    column-gap: clamp(1.25rem, 2.6vw, 2.25rem);
  }
  .pcard--turn .say__t { max-width: none; }
  .turn__lead {
    height: 0;
    align-self: baseline;
    border-bottom: 1px solid color-mix(in srgb, var(--cream) 26%, transparent);
    transform: translateY(-0.26em);
  }
  /* the foot line, at the bottom-left of the frame, inside the corner ramp */
  .turn__foot {
    grid-row: 4;
    align-self: end;
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
    margin: 0;
    max-width: 26ch;
    color: var(--fg-mute);
    padding-bottom: clamp(0.2rem, 0.8vw, 0.6rem);
  }
  /* 03 sets its name at the TOP at poster size, so the top band has to be
     deeper than the bar strip Figure guarantees by default. These are
     Figure's own placement variables, retuned by the scene that needs them —
